PART 3: STATE PATTERNS AND BEST PRACTICES
================================================================================

PATTERN 1: SIMPLE COUNTER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [count, setCount] = useState(0)


const increment = () =>
  setCount(count => count + 1)


const decrement = () =>
  setCount(count => count - 1)


const reset = () =>
  setCount(0)


Use Cases:

• Counting
• Scoring
• Pagination


================================================================================
PATTERN 2: TOGGLE BOOLEAN
================================================================================

const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false)


const toggle = () =>
  setIsOpen(isOpen => !isOpen)


Use Cases:

• Modals
• Dropdowns
• Menus
• Visibility
• Expand/collapse


================================================================================
PATTERN 3: FORM INPUT
================================================================================

const [input, setInput] = useState('')


const handleChange = (e) =>
  setInput(e.target.value)


const handleReset = () =>
  setInput('')


Use Cases:

• Text input
• Search bar
• Filter input


================================================================================
PATTERN 4: OBJECT STATE
================================================================================

const [user, setUser] = useState({
  name: '',
  email: '',
  age: 0
})


const updateUser = (field, value) => {

  setUser(user => ({
    ...user,
    value
  }))
}


Use Cases:

• User profiles
• Settings
• Forms


================================================================================
PATTERN 5: ARRAY STATE
================================================================================

const [items, setItems] = useState([])


const addItem = (item) => {

  setItems(items => [
    ...items,
    item
  ])
}


const removeItem = (id) => {

  setItems(items =>
    items.filter(
      item => item.id !== id
    )
  )
}


Use Cases:

• Lists
• Todos
• Shopping carts
• Collections


================================================================================
PATTERN 6: CONDITIONAL / STATUS STATE
================================================================================

const [status, setStatus] =
  useState('idle')


// Possible values:
// idle
// loading
// success
// error


const startLoading = () =>
  setStatus('loading')


const setSuccess = () =>
  setStatus('success')


const setError = () =>
  setStatus('error')


Use Cases:

• API requests
• Async operations
• Workflows


================================================================================
PATTERN 7: NESTED STATE
================================================================================

const [data, setData] = useState({

  user: {
    name: '',
    email: ''
  },

  settings: {
    theme: 'light',
    notifications: true
  }

})


const updateNested = (section, values) => {

  setData(data => ({

    ...data,

    [section]: {
      ...data[section],
      ...values
    }

  }))
}


Example:

updateNested(
  'user',
  {
    name: 'John'
  }
)


STATE PATTERNS SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

+-------------------+---------------------------+------------+
| PATTERN           | USE CASE                  | COMPLEXITY |
+-------------------+---------------------------+------------+
| Counter           | Counting/pagination       | Low        |
| Toggle            | Modals/menus              | Low        |
| Form Input        | Text input/search         | Low        |
| Object State      | User/settings/forms       | Medium     |
| Array State       | Lists/todos/cart          | Medium     |
| Status State      | Async workflows           | Medium     |
| Nested State      | Hierarchical structures   | High       |
+-------------------+---------------------------+------------+


BEST PRACTICES SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Keep State Minimal
  - Store only information that React needs to remember

• Use Descriptive Names
  - Make state purpose obvious

• Initialize Properly
  - Provide meaningful initial values

• Update Immutably
  - Never directly mutate objects/arrays in state

• Use Functional Updates
  - Especially when the next state depends on previous state

• Group Related State When Helpful
  - Don't group everything automatically

• Avoid Deeply Nested State Where Practical
  - Simplifies immutable updates

• Avoid Unnecessary Prop Drilling
  - Context may help when appropriate

• Validate Input
  - Validate appropriate values before processing/submission

• Handle Errors
  - Provide useful failure states

• Test Behavior
  - Verify UI changes based on state

• Document Complex State
  - Explain non-obvious state structures


