PART 7: STATE PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION
================================================================================

STRATEGY 1: KEEP STATE MINIMAL
================================================================================

Avoid storing everything simply because it exists.

Store information that:

• Must survive renders

and

• Can change independently

and/or

• Affects rendered behavior


Example:

const [user, setUser] = useState({
  name: '',
  email: ''
})


const [isOpen, setIsOpen] =
  useState(false)


const [isLoading, setIsLoading] =
  useState(false)


================================================================================
STRATEGY 2: GROUP OR SPLIT STATE BASED ON HOW IT CHANGES
================================================================================

There is no universal rule that:

"Grouping state is always better"

or:

"Splitting state is always better."


Prefer grouping when fields:

• Belong together
• Commonly update together


Example:

const [form, setForm] = useState({
  name: '',
  email: '',
  password: '',
  confirmPassword: ''
})


Separate unrelated UI status:

const [isSubmitting, setIsSubmitting] =
  useState(false)


const [error, setError] =
  useState(null)


================================================================================
STRATEGY 3: React.memo
================================================================================

import React from 'react'


const UserCard =
  React.memo(function UserCard({
    user
  }) {

    console.log(
      'UserCard rendered'
    )

    return (
      <div>
        {user.name}
      </div>
    )
  })


React.memo can allow React to skip rendering a component when the relevant
props compare as unchanged.

Use it when there is a measurable benefit.


================================================================================
STRATEGY 4: useCallback
================================================================================

import {
  useCallback,
  useState
} from 'react'


function App() {

  const [count, setCount] =
    useState(0)


  const handleClick =
    useCallback(() => {

      setCount(count =>
        count + 1
      )

    }, [])


  return (
    <button onClick={handleClick}>

      Count: {count}

    </button>
  )
}


useCallback can preserve a function reference between renders.

It is most useful when referential stability matters, for example when
passing callbacks to appropriately memoized children.

Do NOT automatically wrap every function in useCallback.


================================================================================
PERFORMANCE STRATEGIES TABLE
================================================================================

+----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------+
| STRATEGY             | BENEFIT                 | USE WHEN                         |
+----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------+
| Minimal State        | Less synchronization    | Generally                        |
| Good State Placement | Limits render scope     | State has local ownership        |
| React.memo           | Can skip child render   | Measured benefit                 |
| useCallback          | Stable function ref     | Referential stability matters    |
| useMemo              | Cache calculation       | Expensive calculation            |
| Lazy Loading         | Defer code/resources    | Large application sections       |
| Pagination           | Limit displayed data    | Large datasets                   |
| Virtualizationnder visible rows     | Very large lists                 |
| Debouncing           | Reduces rapid actions   | Search/input scenarios           |
| Batching             | Coordinates updates     | React update processing          |
+----------------------+-------------------------+----------------------------------+


PERFORMANCE RULE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

MEASURE FIRST.

OPTIMIZE SECOND.

Do not add memoization everywhere by default.


