--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Prefer testing:

USER ACTION
    ↓
VISIBLE RESULT


Instead of testing:

"Was setState called?"


The user cares about application behavior, not the internal Hook call.


================================================================================
PART 9: STATE ANTI-PATTERNS TO AVOID
================================================================================

ANTI-PATTERN 1: DERIVED DATA IN STATE
================================================================================

WRONG:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [firstName, setFirstName] =
  useState('John')

const [lastName, setLastName] =
  useState('Doe')

const [fullName, setFullName] =
  useState('John Doe')


Problem:

fullName duplicates information that can already be calculated.


BETTER:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const fullName =
  `${firstName} ${lastName}`


================================================================================
ANTI-PATTERN 2: MIRRORING PROPS IN STATE WITHOUT A REASON
================================================================================

POTENTIALLY WRONG:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

function UserProfile({
  user
}) {

  const [userData, setUserData] =
    useState(user)

}


Problem:

userData does not automatically synchronize whenever the user prop changes.


BETTER WHEN LOCAL EDITING IS NOT REQUIRED:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

function UserProfile({
  user
}) {

  return (
    <div>
      {user.name}
    </div>
  )
}


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Copying props into state is not universally wrong.

It can be intentional when you need an independent local draft, for example
an edit form.

The important question is:

"Should this local state intentionally diverge from the prop?"


================================================================================
ANTI-PATTERN 3: COMPUTED VALUES IN STATE
================================================================================

POTENTIALLY WRONG:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const [items, setItems] =
  useState([])


const [total, setTotal] =
  useState(0)


If total always equals the sum of item prices, storing both creates
duplicate sources of truth.


BETTER:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const total =
  items.reduce(
    (sum, item) =>
      sum + item.price,
    0
  )


================================================================================
ANTI-PATTERN 4: TOO MANY UNRELATED OR POORLY STRUCTURED STATE VALUES
================================================================================

This is NOT automatically wrong:

const [name, setName] = useState('')
const [email, setEmail] = useState('')
const [phone, setPhone] = useState('')


Separate state can be appropriate.


However, a related form can also be grouped:

const [formData, setFormData] =
  useState({

    name: '',

    email: '',

    phone: '',

    address: '',

    city: '',

    state: '',

    zip: ''

  })


RULE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Choose a state structure based on how the values logically belong together
and how they are updated.


================================================================================
ANTI-PATTERN 5: STORING MORE API DATA THAN NEEDED
================================================================================

RAW:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

const response =
  await fetch(...)

const body =
  await response.json()


If the UI needs only:

• id
• name

you can map the API representation into the application's data model.


Example:

const user = {

  id:
    body.data.id,

  name:
    body.data.name

}


This reduces coupling between UI code and external response structure.


================================================================================
ANTI-PATTERNS TABLE
================================================================================

+--------------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------+
| ANTI-PATTERN             | PROBLEM                 | BETTER APPROACH           |
+--------------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------+
| Derived data in state    | Can become inconsistent | Calculate from source     |
| Unneeded prop mirror     | Can become stale        | Use prop directly         |
| Duplicate computed state | Multiple sources truth  | Derive value              |
| Poor state structure     | Harder updates          | Group/split thoughtfully  |
| Raw API coupling         | UI tied to API format   | Map required fields       |
| State mirrors props      | Synchronization issue   | Keep only when intentional|
| Functions in state       | Usually unnecessary     | Define function normally  |
| DOM refs in state        | Causes unnecessary work | useRef                    |
| Globalizing local state  | Excessive shared state  | Keep state close to owner |
+--------------------------+-------------------------+---------------------------+


