COMMON PATTERNS TO USE
================================================================================

COUNTER:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setCount(count =>
  count + 1
)


TOGGLE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setIsOpen(isOpen =>
  !isOpen
)


FORM:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setInput(
  e.target.value
)


OBJECT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setUser(user => ({

  ...user,

  name: value

}))


ARRAY ADD:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setItems(items => [
  ...items,
  newItem
])


ARRAY REMOVE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setItems(items =>
  items.filter(
    item =>
      item.id !== id
  )
)


ARRAY UPDATE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setItems(items =>
  items.map(item =>

    item.id === id
      ? {
          ...item,
          property: value
        }
      : item

  )
)


NESTED STATE:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

setState(state => ({

  ...state,

  nested: {

    ...state.nested,

    property: value

  }

}))


STATUS:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

'idle'
   ↓
'loading'
   ↓
'success'

or:

'error'


================================================================================
ANTI-PATTERNS TO AVOID
================================================================================

AVOID:

• Direct state mutation

• Mutating arrays with push(), splice(), sort(), reverse()

• Duplicating easily derived values in state

• Mirroring props in state without a clear requirement

• Deeply nesting state without a reason

• Using index as a key when list identity can change

• Expecting the state variable to change inside the current handler
  immediately after calling its setter

• await setState(...)

• Globalizing state that belongs locally

• Memoizing everything prematurely

• Storing DOM element references in state when useRef is appropriate


